== ws
set up the server and the Mac that works on it

Usage:
  ws [flags]
  ws [command]

Available Commands:
  client      the client's systems
  help        Help about any command
  server      the server's systems
  update      update ws to the latest release
  version     print the version of this ws

Flags:
  -h, --help    help for ws
      --plain   print lines, with no full-screen display

Use "ws [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws client
the client's systems

Usage:
  ws client [flags]
  ws client [command]

Available Commands:
  macos       the commands for a Mac

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for client

Global Flags:
      --plain   print lines, with no full-screen display

Use "ws client [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws client macos
the commands for a Mac

Usage:
  ws client macos [flags]
  ws client macos [command]

Available Commands:
  apply       set the Mac up from mac.conf and a server's file
  conf        print the checked settings of mac.conf and a server's file as shell assignments
  configure   edit mac.conf, the server's file, the secrets and ws's host_vars on forms
  remove      remove a server from the Mac
  resolver    send DOMAIN's names to the nameservers, or drop it
  secrets     edit ws's secrets file, over ssh
  servers     list the Mac's servers with their target, slot and tunnel
  state       report what the Mac's steps would change
  tunnel      the Mac's tunnel to ws
  wizard      set the Mac up in stages

Flags:
  -h, --help            help for macos
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one

Global Flags:
      --plain   print lines, with no full-screen display

Use "ws client macos [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws client macos apply
It runs the steps it names, from mac.conf and the server's file, in this order: ssh, cable, git, ghostty, zed, cld, zsh, tunnel, networks. With no --only, it runs every step, and ssh again, whose plan is empty unless cld changed ssh's file. It asks sudo where it needs root.

Usage:
  ws client macos apply [flags]

Flags:
      --check          print each change and make none
  -h, --help           help for apply
      --only strings   run the named steps alone, as a list or by repeating the flag

Global Flags:
      --plain           print lines, with no full-screen display
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos conf
It reads FILE, else $WS_CONF, else ~/.config/ws/mac.conf, and the server's file, servers/NAME/server.conf beside it, of the server that --server names, and prints the checked settings as shell assignments, or the errors of a file it refuses.

Usage:
  ws client macos conf [FILE] [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help    help for conf
      --lines   check each line alone, as a wizard checks an answer before the file holds its key

Global Flags:
      --plain           print lines, with no full-screen display
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos configure
It shows the keys that ws client macos wizard would ask, by WS_TARGET, with what the files hold: mac.conf's, then those of the server's file, servers/NAME/server.conf. It writes them on Ctrl-S. Ctrl-N goes on to ws's secrets, which show as set or not, never their values, then to ws's host_vars, both read over ssh. It needs a terminal: anywhere else, edit the file by hand.

Usage:
  ws client macos configure [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for configure

Global Flags:
      --plain           print lines, with no full-screen display
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos remove
It deletes servers/NAME beside mac.conf and NAME's entry in known_hosts, and leaves the server as it is. It refuses the Mac's last server, as apply needs one, and takes no --server: the argument is the consent.

Usage:
  ws client macos remove NAME [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for remove

Global Flags:
      --plain           print lines, with no full-screen display
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos resolver
It has macOS ask the nameservers for DOMAIN, or drop the file of DOMAIN where it gets none.

Usage:
  ws client macos resolver DOMAIN [ADDRESS...] [flags]

Flags:
      --check   print each change and make none
  -h, --help    help for resolver

Global Flags:
      --plain           print lines, with no full-screen display
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
